A||||36||Spektrum-87GT||disgusted with exotic car companies||||1||
Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||10-14-2001||02:27 PM||||this might belong in o/t, but i think it applies<BR> <A HREF="http://www.kitcarmagazine.com/Lamborghini.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kitcarmagazine.com/Lamborghini.htm</A> <P>ok, first ferrari does it, now lamborghini is doing it. attempting to shut down kit car manufacturers, this makes me sick. Not everyone can afford a 200k+ lamborghini or a 150k+ ferrari. So we do the next best thing, build a car that resembles the look, because not all of us make millions a year and have that kind of money to spend on a car. Its not like IFG was making cars and selling them as lamborghinis. They call the the phantom and interceptor and post that they're replicars all over their site. What this is, is greed. Exotic cars make their money off of the simple laws of supply and demand. They only send over a few cars every year, and EVERYONE wants one. They can charge as much as they'd like, and they'll sell them. As many other people, I love the looks of the diablo and countach, but will NEVER be able to afford one off of 20k a year and dont want a car that has such high maintainence. I was planning on building a kit car sometime within the next 5 years, but it looks like that wont be happening now. I hope IFG steps up and doesnt back down. Hopefully if they win, companies can start making ferrari kit cars again too. IFG has my support and I hope has everyone elses too.||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||10-14-2001||02:42 PM||||Just my opinion, but I think direct "knock offs" should only be allowed if the company is no longer in business of producing the car.<P>As far as the Fiero goes, it's a timeless design, why bother trying to change it much?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||10-14-2001||06:47 PM||||Im workin on getting my Lambo before that all happens as we speak.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||10-14-2001||06:58 PM||||Well, fiero kits are Obviously copying off exotic cars. Except for Archie's fino/finnali(sp?), and irm... I dont know of any orignal fiero bodys. All of them are basicly ferrari rip offs. Yes, most people can spot a kit cars from a mile away. But they still are copies!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steve Normington||10-14-2001||06:59 PM||||I'm on the side of Ferrari. They developed a body style and therefore have the rights to that body shape. If someone copies their body work and markets a product, that company should be punished. When you develop something, you have the rights to produce and sell it. Why was everyone against the Fiero Store when they seemed to copy an idea? Because we knew it was wrong. It is wrong to do that to Ferrari.<BR>[/rant]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||10-14-2001||07:16 PM||||The following is the opinion of Cooter and in no way should reflect the overall opinions of other members:<BR>If a copy causes the original designing party to lose the sale of a new piece of equipment, then the party selling the copy is wrong. (Ex.- the Fiero Store copying Rodney's kit)<BR>If the person/persons/company making a copy does not cause the original manufactoring company to lose the sell of a new piece of equipment, then the original manufactoring company has lost no revenue and should have no issue with someone selling a copy. (Ex.-I will never have the $$$$ for a real Lambo, so I buy a kit car. The Lambo company has not lost anything because I would have never made that purchase anyway.)<BR>Just another opinion to add to the list.||||reg||1
Z||000006||firstfiero||10-14-2001||07:24 PM||||    Yea I think it's great that you can pick up a perfect 50 dollar version of my 4000 dollar Rolex..... NOT! All replica's suck!<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||87FieroGTx||10-14-2001||07:36 PM|||| I think its cool, I would love to see more orignal fiero rebodys like K-1's Camaro/Honda rebodies.<P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT ~ auto 3.2 200hp 220tq ~ 75 shot NOS ~ 0-60 4.8 13.9 1/4||||reg||1
Z||000008||GTFiero1||10-14-2001||07:46 PM||||my opinion is just to let them make replicas. There is no substitute for an original so if some one had the money for a real lamborghini the'd by it, they wouldnt settle for a replica. Replica companies are thinking about the little guy. The average american. The lamborghini you saw in that movie last week...replica, that ferrari...replica. why would a movie company risk wrecking a $200,000 when they can risk wrecking a $20,000 car. I do like origanal designs more though.<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||10-14-2001||07:54 PM||||LOL@firstfiero! My Rolex was only $25- bought it from a guy at the baseball stadium selling watches from his coat. D@mn thing lasted 15 years before it even needed a battery. The jewelry store refused to fix it though- I guess kind of like taking your replicar to a Ferarri dealer to have the oil changed   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||10-14-2001||11:11 PM||||They have every right to take legal action against replica makers. They own the copyrights to the designs. It's really not much different that pirating sowtware or downloading a song from Napster. The only difference is that when you pirate music or sowtware you are getting an <B>exact</B> copy. In the past Lamborghini has been confident that thier product was far superior to any replica, but now under new ownership it looks like things have changed. You mentioned supply and demand, well the easier it is to get a replica, the less demand there is for the real thing. Sure it's true that most people who consider a real one do not consider a kit and vice-versa, but there are a few that do, and with lamborghini only making a handfull of cars per year it does make a difference in their sales. When I picked up my kit there were some pretty high class customers pulling up in a Mercedes to look at replicas.<BR> The really sad part of all this is that when Lamborghini (or Ferrari) takes action and puts companies out of business, the quality of the kits goes way down. With several companies producing the same body style there is competition, and when there is competition the product is improved. If there is only one guy left making Lambo kits, he has no incentive to make a good quality product because what else will the consumer do, they have no where else to go, except to Lamborghini. The manufacturers are also forced to do business in ways that are less than legal, and this creates a great oportunity for them to rip people off. <BR> No matter what happens, you will still be able to buy a replica of your favorite exotic, they will just be harder to find. They shut down Napster, but that doesn't mean you can't download music anymore, you just have to look harder. Ferrari kits are still being made, it's just kept a little bit quieter. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrarisues.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cooter||10-14-2001||11:40 PM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackStang95||10-15-2001||01:46 AM||||I think ferrari and lambourghini are totally wrong for pursuing this because it in no way hurts their sales or profit.  No one who could afford a real one would build a kit...period.  So why kill it?  ||||reg||4
Z||000013||FLASHY||10-15-2001||01:59 AM||||If I were Ferrari or Lamborghini I wouldn't worry about these kits affecting their sales. In fact, they MAY help their sales. The more people see them on the road the more they'd like to have one for themselves. The kits almost form an advertisement for the company. I understand that it can also be looked in another way whereas those with the big bucks to buy the real thing wouldn't feel as special if more of them were driving around.<P>I dunno, I wish it could stay the way it was where us monetary challenged folks had the chance to own their dream cars without having to pay the big price. Selfish as it may be, that's the only way I could own a Lambo.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/spinsig3.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||DKOV||10-15-2001||03:51 AM||||Rockcrawl...<P>I know Vui and even though Ferrari dumped its high dollar attorneys and have effectively forced him into cooperating, I think that the press coverage over the incident is biased to Ferrari...<P>Vui is in the business of repairing and restoring cars.  If anyone went to a capable bodyshop to replace a damaged fender or bonnet on any car, a bodyshop would never turn them down.  In this case, well... three of them, ASSEMBLY was performed.  It's not Vui's fault or decision on WHAT was assembled and what "decorations" were provided him to attach.<P>The disput was wether or not Vui was manufacturing the body to begin with.  For the press to say that he denies building those three cars... that's misleading.  Sure he assembled them.  He stated that.<P>That aside, I think there is a double edged sword in the lawsuits going on in this industry...  More Ferrari's on the road, real or not, is just plain GOOD for Ferrari.  Any business owner in the world would accept free advertising.<P>What?  Robbing Ferrari of the money they might make if that owner were to have bought a REAL version?  I think Ferrari needs to re-check its demographics.  Strangely, those individuals buying REAL Ferrari's are NOT the same people buying replicas.  <P>I'm sure that Bill Gates is sitting at home thinking...  "Hmmm.  I've got more Money than God and I want to buy a new car.  Hmmm...  I can buy a REAL Ferrari with all the benefits that come with it.  Things like Warranty, Service, Support, Status.<P>OR<P>I can buy a trashed Fiero, stuff a V8 into it, learn to weld, paint and perform 1000 other machanical duties that mechanics don't even want to do, learn to work fiberglass, prep and paint, spend a bit more than a year of constant work and finally, if I don't quit on the project, drive my Replica to a party with all my friends (that own REAL Ferrari's) so they can point out that it is FAKE"<P>Let's be realistic.  Ferrari isn't even MAKING most of these bodies any longer.  Ferrari, the Manufacturer, has ALREADY SOLD all the models and collected all the funds they ever will.  They people that are loosing any money are the USED CAR SALESMEN that are peddling these used exotics.  Why aren't they suing?  Because they know the difference between THEIR customers and the Kit Car purchaser.<P>The other side of the coin is bodies that ARE in production...  This, I feel, it wrong.  If the model is current, it's just good manners and respect to allows every possible opportunity to those that have spent the money and time to develope the car.<P>After all... who's suing Shelby for building the NEW Cobra, when he "replicated" the design in the first place?<P>Just my .02 worth  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000015||sloth85GT||10-15-2001||05:04 AM||||Copying is a form of flattery. I am siding with the little guys on this one. Lamborghini is not losing any money over this. Not everyone wants to just buy a sports car, people like to build their cars. And if they can do that and save $140,000 while doing it, GREAT! If someone can't tell the difference between a Lamborghini and a replicar, then they need one bullet to the skull. And Lambo doesn't even make the Countach anymore, so why would they care about that? Someone above made a comment that some guys consider buying the real deal but decide not to because they can just make a replicar. I don't agree with that, if I was a millionaire, I wouldn't settle for a kit car, it's not even close in performance, it just looks like the real thing, yeah most people have v8's in them, but it's not a Lambo V-12!!! My point is, replicars aren't even close to the real thing and they aren't robbing the super car companies of any money. They're just being greedy bastards!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">     <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||firstfiero||10-15-2001||06:58 AM||||   LoL...That doesn't surprise me cooter. Rolex is another company that will go after anyone working on or selling Fakes. You going in for a battery was a dead giveaway it's a fake. All Rolexs are Self winding. I believe they invented the self winding watch. Anyway as soon as you said you needed a battery any jeweler that knows anything will turn you away. <BR>   I would have to agree with the statement that replica buyers and authentic buyers are two different customers. Someone who can blow 4 grand for a watch didn't consider buying one off the guy for 50 bucks instead and vice versa.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||mshill||10-15-2001||10:27 AM||||I bought my Rolex in Mexico for $12.  Lasted 3 months.  That's the difference between a real and a copy.  I am building an F50 look alike.  It is not a replica (7 inches too short) and will not carry Ferrari badging either.  It will be my custom Fiero with custom badging.  I couldn't afford to have a real Italian car tuned-up let alone purchase, insure, and maintain one.  <P>I too would like to see more original designs, but there is a reason why the Ferraris and  Lambos are copied so much, they are simply stunning designs.||||reg||1
Z||000018||88 Fiero||10-15-2001||10:31 AM||||Well...if Ferrari and Lamborghini manage to put all kit cars mfg out of business...there's still hope. Country like the UK are still in business making Ferrari kit cars. For example...the Fiero Factory in UK makes several Ferrari replicas.. For instance,I called long distance to this company in UK and inquire about their kits for the 355. The gentleman that I talk to gave me the information that I need. I also asked him whether Ferrari has tried to shut them down. He said that Ferrari tried but in the UK, there is no law that would allow Ferrari to shut them down and that Ferrari doesnt have any leverage.. So I take that as a good thing...||||reg||12
Z||000019||Screwie||10-15-2001||11:36 AM||||Weird idea, but Ferrari and Lamborghini could start their own kitcar business! Make the best out of a difficult situation   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Screwie (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000020||FieroBUZZ||10-15-2001||11:44 AM||||I have to agree that no one should make copies of a currently produced model.  Besides which, these companies have gone to a considerable expense to copyright their design.<P>If you regard any lost business as a yardstick, if I were a multi millionaire living down the street from Cooter I wouldn't buy the real thing just because he has a knock off.  Ergo lost sale.<P>If these guys are talented enough to design a kit, surely they can design an original kit.<BR>Gary<P>||||reg||12
Z||000021||Steve Normington||10-15-2001||11:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroBUZZ:<BR><B>If you regard any lost business as a yardstick, if I were a multi millionaire living down the street from Cooter I wouldn't buy the real thing just because he has a knock off.  Ergo lost sale.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I agree. What if Bill Gates thinks, "I'd like to buy a Ferrari, but it won't get me prestige because people will assume it is a replica."? One of the reasons people buy these cars (or Rolexes) is so people think, "he has a lot of money." If the sight of a Ferrari becomes common due to kit cars, people will not think that.<P>Also on a similar vein, I've noticed that "big company bad, small company (or individual) good" seems to be the decision making process for many people. Not too much here, but many places. [/rant]||||reg||1
Z||000022||DKOV||10-15-2001||12:24 PM||||Something else occured to me this morning...<P>I, pehaps like many, have the perspective on looking that the "replica argument" from both sides...  I own a Lotus Esprit Turbo.  Not a Ferrari but an Exotic Super Car none the less...  I am ALSO the proud owner of a Mirage Ferrari 355 Replica.<P>The Lotus is the REAL DEAL.  Is fast.  Is Awsome.  Is Exotic.  Is COMPLETLEY foreign to me!  I don't have the faintest idea of how to work on the darn thing!  It might as well have come from Mars!<P>On the other hand, my Ferrari 355 replica is know to me INSIDE & OUT.  Every bolt, every nut.  If I hear anything, I can tell what's happening.  If I need to fix anything, I can.<P>The BIGGEST reward to my kit project (which is NOT completed yet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) is that I also have the priviledge of the BUILDING EXPERIENCE and the rights to "Brag" about that process.<P>The closes I'll ever get to tha with my Lotus is a ground up restoration that will be done by a certified Lotus mechanic anyway.  But I did buy the Lotus because it <B>IS</B> a Lotus.  Plain and Simple.  Regardless of the quality of the kit, there is NO substitue for the real thing.<P>For me, this is a BIG advantage to a replica car... of ANY type.<P>Maybe Ferrari, Lamborghini and Lotus should have "customer day" where the perspective buyer flys in and spends a year and a half in the factory building their own car, and then be able to buy it for cheap because the new owner's labor offset the cost!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000023||BlackStang95||10-15-2001||03:08 PM||||A possible solution...if lambo or ferrari were smart, they could sell competitively priced replica parts themselves.  Think about it...every kit car person could then claim it was a real lambourghini, i think theyd easily outsell any of the other makers out there.  just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000024||artherd||10-15-2001||03:33 PM||||The proper thing to do (and what usually is done...) is to wait untill a car is OUT of production before you choose to 'rip it off' and build a half-ass hack-job conversion onto a crappy Fiero.<P><BR>I mean, come on.  If Diahatsu build a 'Fee-ero' that looked like our cars, only had a 3-cylinder 800cc engine, weighed more, and had only automatic transmissions, looked like a Fiero only older and with even MORE mishapen pannels and other insults, would WE stand for it?!  <P>I mean, honestly, what's with all these 2.8 automatic 'you wish Ferrari 308s' out there? (no offence to any owners, I just find it disturbing.) I'd be offended too if I went to the trouble of building, say, the Georgous and devine mechanical and musical masterpiece that is the 360 Modena, only to have some hack try and fail so poorly to immitate me.<P>Now, if someone were to say take all the interior bits from a 360, build a body lovingly by hand to match, put it all on a Fiero, and drop in a 405bhp Corvette LT5 4-cam aluminum V8 and build a car that could at least accelerate with, if not quite outrun, the real deal, then now we'd be talking... (but ONLY after my 360 has moved out of production, DAMNIT!!!)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000025||Formula88||10-15-2001||03:43 PM||||Well, IMHO, the problem is copyright, not lost revenue.  Ferrari and Lamborghini know that kit cars aren't a business threat or competition to them.  But the kit cars are using their name, their logos, their designs on work that is not theirs.  That's like you writing your own software and putting the Microsoft Logo on it.  Doesn't matter if MS doesn't sell the exact same thing.  The logo use is copyright infringement.  <P>The use of the Ferrari emblems on a non-Ferrari product could tarnish the Ferrari name.  At least, I'd think that would be their argument.  People in the know can spot a kit car vs. the genuine article, but what about someone who's never seen a Ferrari in person?  That kit car that they just saw and thought was a real Ferrari has just given them an image of what a Ferrari is.  An image that Ferrari didn't create or condone.<P>Personally, I want to do a Ferrari rebody, but I'm going to put Pegasus emblems on it.  It will either say "Fiero" or it will not have any name on it at all.  It won't have any genuine Ferrari parts on it, or at least, no Ferrari copyrighted logos, etc.  ||||reg||6
Z||000026||Formula88||10-15-2001||03:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Think about it...every kit car person could then claim it was a real lambourghini, i think theyd easily outsell any of the other makers out there.  just a thought.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And that is exactly the reason why Lamborghini or Ferrari will NEVER do that.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Master_Sushi||10-15-2001||04:08 PM||||{{Also on a similar vein, I've noticed that "big company bad, small company (or individual) good" seems to be the decision making process for many people}} - By: Steve Normington<P><BR>I just have to note this. Its so very true. I get sick of the typical Libral view of how evil big companies are. In allot of ways they have it harder then the small ones!||||reg||14
Z||000028||rogergarrison||10-15-2001||05:30 PM||||I just posted a rebody in another topic thats completely original. I like it myself. I like having the replica myself because it still has the super car look that everyone identifies with and the ease (ok Fiero stretches that) of maintainence of US brands. I hope to be a 3 car family soon with my Ferrari rebody, and working on getting a Lambo with a tube frame. Id never buy either of them real just for the reason of there 'UN' reliability. I havent had to spend $500 in 4 years for any maintainence. Try that with a real one. (ie timing belts and major tune up every 15K for $4,000). Having the real one is no consolation when its sitting broke down all the time. I had a friend that had a real Countach and everytime he took it out something broke. There was a guy in the paper a year or so ago that sued the dealership to take back his Diablo because, the same thing happened to him after paying $240,000. I think Im doing them a favor, giving them free positive advertising.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Spektrum-87GT||10-15-2001||06:12 PM||||countach/diablo is no longer made. Neither are a lot of the old ferrari kits. I agree with your last paragraph, Archie's kits rule!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroBUZZ:<BR><B>I have to agree that no one should make copies of a currently produced model.  Besides which, these companies have gone to a considerable expense to copyright their design.<P>If these guys are talented enough to design a kit, surely they can design an original kit.<BR>Gary<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||FieroKyle||10-15-2001||11:52 PM||||Personally I would never put a kit on my car, except for the IMSA widebody kit. I don't want my car to be an imitation, just something of its own. IMSA Fiero kit is a Fiero, just the racing style kit.||||reg||1
Z||000031||FLASHY||10-16-2001||01:41 AM||||Sorry I didn't post this here, I know I should have but I was too excited and couldn't think straight!! But there's hope for us Lambo kit lovers.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011051.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011051.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000032||Jay||10-16-2001||12:52 PM||||The issue is simple.  Ferrari owns the rights to their cars and can do whatever they want with someone who copies.  As far as not selling to the same people as the real deal, that is just foolish thinking.  A kit car, done correctly with body, engine, interior will run you upwards of 20-30 000 dollars, not to mention your time and effort.  A used Ferrari will cost the same 308,328.  I know someone who has a real 328 and a 328 kit, he bought the kit first.  The kit car industry hurts Ferrari and Lamborghini because they have always made 1 less car than they have orders for.  For instance, they had 400 orders for the F40 but they only made 399.  If you see a Ferrari every day it is no longer special and that is what they are trying to protect.<P>I've owned a kit car (246 dino), but you know what?  For all the money and time I sank into it I could be driving the real car right now.  As far as repairs on the real deal go, they are not as expensive as you think.  Okay maybe they are if you go to the dealer to have the work done.  But parts for Ferraris are also found on Fiats, Alfa Romeos and Lancia's.  You never ask for an alternator for a Ferrari, you ask for the part number and say it is from an Alfa.<P>My suggestion is to keep your Fiero looking like a Fiero and if you want a real Ferrari go out and buy one.  If you look long enough you can find one in your range.  That's what I'm doing.  BTW my Rolex doesn't have a battery either ($5000 Stainless date perpetual). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||ChopTop||10-16-2001||02:54 PM||||I’m ambivalent on this issue, it seems to be more about class warfare than of copyright law.  One on hand, I can understand why a car company who may have spent decades establishing a brand and a reputation would get upset with individuals who make copies of their product.  On the other hand , Ferrari is partly responsible for this problem.  They have knowingly cultivated a product for people to covet.  In essence becoming victims of their own success.  What is also ironic about this debate is while Ferrari doesn’t like people owning/building replicars based on their designs, they don’t have a problem with licensing their prancing horse to vendors who affix it to dubious products in order to sell it to thousands of people who can’t afford the very product it represents.<BR>In addition, Ferrari does make money off the replicar market by selling original Ferrari parts to people building replicars.  Pick up any kit car magazine and you will find vendors ads offering original Ferrari, badges, lights, grills, interiors, etc.  I find it difficult to believe Ferrari isn’t aware of this market and hasn’t taken the necessary steps to end it.  As long as there is a market for Ferrari replicars, people will continue to find ways (legal & illegall) to produce them.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||ChopTop||10-16-2001||02:56 PM||||I’m ambivalent on this issue, it seems to be more about class warfare than of copyright law.  One the one hand, I can understand why a car company who may have spent decades establishing a brand and a reputation would get upset with individuals who make copies of their product.  On the other, Ferrari is partly responsible for this problem.  They have knowingly cultivated a product for people to covet.  In essence becoming victims of their own success.  What is also ironic about this debate is while Ferrari doesn’t like people owning/building replicars based on their designs, they don’t have a problem with licensing their prancing horse to vendors who affix it to dubious products in order to sell it to thousands of people who can’t afford the very product it represents.<BR>In addition, Ferrari does make money off the replicar market by selling original Ferrari parts to people building replicars.  Pick up any kit car magazine and you will find vendors ads offering original Ferrari, badges, lights, grills, interiors, etc.  I find it difficult to believe Ferrari isn’t aware of this market and hasn’t taken the necessary steps to end it.  As long as there is a market for Ferrari replicars, people will continue to find ways (legal & illegall) to produce them.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000035||rogergarrison||10-16-2001||05:20 PM||||Thats true, never thought of that angle. I wont buy the real Ferrari, even though i could have had one for prob $10,000 less than Ive got in mine. Because I 'have' one, i buy all kinds of authorized Ferrari stuff. Dozens of diecast, watches, house clocks, clothing (t shirts, sweatshirts, coats) luggage, wallet, flags, posters and thats not to mention all the 'real' expensive parts on my car that I got from my local Ferrari dealer. If I hadnt built my kit, I wouldnt have bought any of it.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Spektrum-87GT||10-17-2001||06:05 PM||||just read the other post and i think this thread is over, thank goodness. I completely agree with ChopTop on every point he made. I also noticed that the 'letter', fake or not, was sent out in march. IFG hasnt even blinked since then and pushed forward with they're new kit. I'm not sure how respectable of a company IFG is, I know they used to be crap, but maybe they've changed they're ways.||||reg||1
